1
00:03:15,128 --> 00:03:45,108
Alright, let's go on.

2
00:03:45,128 --> 00:04:15,108
We'll be right back.

3
00:04:15,128 --> 00:04:45,108
We'll be right back.

4
00:04:45,128 --> 00:04:49,428
Secret Bond! Iceberg and Frankie!

5
00:05:03,208 --> 00:05:04,328
Storm's coming, huh?

6
00:05:04,588 --> 00:05:06,888
Better finish this up before the wind gets even worse.

7
00:05:06,888 --> 00:05:21,608
What's he doing?

8
00:05:24,888 --> 00:05:27,328
Give it back!

9
00:05:27,328 --> 00:05:38,868
We need the money to fix the berry.

10
00:05:40,008 --> 00:05:41,808
Her scars are deep.

11
00:05:41,808 --> 00:05:46,028
That's our money!

12
00:05:46,328 --> 00:05:48,868
It's to heal her wounds, understand me?

13
00:05:49,168 --> 00:05:50,768
You can't have it!

14
00:05:51,408 --> 00:05:53,808
Give it back to me right now!

15
00:05:53,808 --> 00:05:58,088
Oh, I see now.

16
00:05:58,188 --> 00:06:01,108
That must be the ship he needed the money to have repaired he was talking about.

17
00:06:01,248 --> 00:06:02,008
What a disaster.

18
00:06:02,348 --> 00:06:06,128
Yeah, even if we didn't come here to nab him, Aqualaguna would still wash him away.

19
00:06:06,928 --> 00:06:08,428
Talk about bad luck.

20
00:06:08,428 --> 00:06:12,428
Okay.

21
00:06:13,068 --> 00:06:17,248
I had just enough lumber to patch up the rough spot, so it should hold even if it's not pretty.

22
00:06:18,408 --> 00:06:20,308
You're wasting your time, pal.

23
00:06:22,848 --> 00:06:23,548
Who's there?

24
00:06:24,168 --> 00:06:25,808
Come on, you're kidding me, right?

25
00:06:26,048 --> 00:06:29,068
Or did we beat you so bad you already forgot who I am?

26
00:06:29,488 --> 00:06:31,108
Pull it together, man.

27
00:06:32,008 --> 00:06:32,728
Oh, no!

28
00:06:32,848 --> 00:06:33,868
Anybody but him!

29
00:06:34,728 --> 00:06:35,628
What do you want?

30
00:06:35,908 --> 00:06:37,108
Leave me alone, you bastard!

31
00:06:37,108 --> 00:06:37,768
Now, now.

32
00:06:37,888 --> 00:06:39,268
Let's all calm down.

33
00:06:39,528 --> 00:06:42,388
As long as you play it cool, I won't have to hurt you.

34
00:06:44,228 --> 00:06:47,568
Even I'm not crazy enough to fight a guy in the middle of this storm.

35
00:06:48,028 --> 00:06:52,448
But if we don't want to sink, it looks like I'll have to take that ship of yours hostage as well.

36
00:06:52,928 --> 00:06:54,328
What are you talking about?

37
00:06:54,808 --> 00:06:58,648
Well, I got a score to settle with Straw Hat, you see.

38
00:06:58,648 --> 00:07:01,328
So I came here to kidnap you.

39
00:07:19,768 --> 00:07:22,848
I used the backside.

40
00:07:23,388 --> 00:07:24,068
They're fine.

41
00:07:24,228 --> 00:07:26,968
I hope you're proud of yourself beating those poor shipwrights!

42
00:07:26,968 --> 00:07:28,308
You're so strong, Zoro!

43
00:07:28,928 --> 00:07:29,408
Whatever.

44
00:07:30,108 --> 00:07:31,428
Might as well go ahead.

45
00:07:32,168 --> 00:07:33,328
Zoro, this way!

46
00:07:33,548 --> 00:07:34,108
Got it!

47
00:07:34,588 --> 00:07:34,908
No!

48
00:07:35,008 --> 00:07:35,748
Over here!

49
00:07:36,028 --> 00:07:37,928
We're leading the way for you, stupid!

50
00:07:38,068 --> 00:07:39,668
How can you get lost already?

51
00:07:39,948 --> 00:07:40,488
Oh, here.

52
00:07:41,228 --> 00:07:42,028
Zoro's gone!

53
00:07:42,108 --> 00:07:43,648
I think we lost it forever!

54
00:07:44,088 --> 00:07:45,348
We're doing this on purpose!

55
00:07:45,668 --> 00:07:46,968
Zoro, where did you go?!

56
00:07:46,968 --> 00:07:47,248
Zoro!

57
00:07:47,248 --> 00:07:47,288
Zoro!

58
00:07:47,288 --> 00:07:47,328
Zoro!

59
00:07:47,328 --> 00:07:47,388
Zoro!

60
00:07:47,388 --> 00:07:47,888
Zoro!

61
00:07:47,888 --> 00:07:48,328
Zoro!

62
00:07:48,328 --> 00:07:48,888
Zoro!

63
00:07:48,888 --> 00:07:49,328
Zoro!

64
00:07:49,328 --> 00:07:49,448
Zoro!

65
00:07:49,448 --> 00:07:49,488
Zoro!

66
00:07:49,488 --> 00:07:49,888
Zoro!

67
00:07:49,888 --> 00:07:50,008
Zoro!

68
00:07:50,008 --> 00:07:50,448
Zoro!

69
00:07:50,448 --> 00:07:50,488
Zoro!

70
00:07:50,488 --> 00:07:51,008
Zoro!

71
00:07:51,008 --> 00:07:51,488
Zoro!

72
00:07:51,488 --> 00:07:51,568
Zoro!

73
00:07:51,568 --> 00:07:52,008
Zoro!

74
00:07:52,008 --> 00:07:53,008
Zoro!

75
00:07:53,008 --> 00:07:53,048
Zoro!

76
00:07:53,048 --> 00:07:53,568
Zoro!

77
00:07:53,568 --> 00:07:54,008
Zoro!

78
00:07:54,008 --> 00:07:55,008
Zoro!

79
00:07:55,008 --> 00:07:56,088
Zoro!

80
00:07:56,088 --> 00:07:56,528
Zoro!

81
00:07:56,528 --> 00:07:57,148
Zoro!

82
00:08:02,148 --> 00:08:02,768
See?

83
00:08:02,768 --> 00:08:04,108
I can do that stuff!

84
00:08:04,108 --> 00:08:05,228
Because I'm rubber!

85
00:08:05,228 --> 00:08:05,688
OK.

86
00:08:05,688 --> 00:08:06,368
That's good!

87
00:08:06,368 --> 00:08:08,308
Now do that with the rest of your body.

88
00:08:08,308 --> 00:08:08,928
Sure!

89
00:08:08,928 --> 00:08:09,868
Just give me a sec!

90
00:08:09,868 --> 00:08:33,988
Easy, cause I'm rubber!

91
00:08:34,788 --> 00:08:37,528
Alright, good, now help me!

92
00:08:37,528 --> 00:08:38,388
No prob!

93
00:08:39,868 --> 00:08:45,708
You're going to pay, you masked bastards!

94
00:08:51,388 --> 00:08:54,908
It's true, we've been undercover for the last five years.

95
00:08:55,668 --> 00:09:00,028
Ah, but don't worry, we took our jobs as shipwrights very seriously.

96
00:09:02,188 --> 00:09:04,488
I understand how shocked you must be.

97
00:09:05,048 --> 00:09:08,088
However, we can't devote any more time to this matter.

98
00:09:08,088 --> 00:09:11,528
We will achieve our goal by any means necessary.

99
00:09:11,528 --> 00:09:17,368
So if you're thinking about resisting for the sake of being difficult, I suggest you reconsider.

100
00:09:17,368 --> 00:09:19,768
For your own good as well as ours.

101
00:09:19,768 --> 00:09:24,408
I assume you're already familiar with CypherPol.

102
00:09:25,208 --> 00:09:28,408
It's the government's supreme intelligence agency with eight branches.

103
00:09:28,948 --> 00:09:32,848
Their worldwide bases are referred to as CP1 through CP8.

104
00:09:33,588 --> 00:09:37,828
Together, they serve as the government's eyes and ears in every corner of the globe.

105
00:09:38,328 --> 00:09:39,288
Of course I know.

106
00:09:39,288 --> 00:09:42,168
But how do all of you fit into that picture?

107
00:09:42,728 --> 00:09:43,528
Good question.

108
00:09:44,168 --> 00:09:47,568
As the ninth CypherPol unit, we officially do not exist.

109
00:09:48,008 --> 00:09:52,388
And by virtue of that, we possess a certain privilege that the other branches lack.

110
00:09:52,808 --> 00:09:55,028
One that necessitates a degree of secrecy.

111
00:09:55,128 --> 00:09:55,728
A privilege?

112
00:09:56,948 --> 00:09:57,848
That's right.

113
00:09:58,348 --> 00:10:01,308
Consider it a blade we wield in the name of justice.

114
00:10:01,308 --> 00:10:08,468
If a citizen is uncooperative towards the government, we may execute them.

115
00:10:09,808 --> 00:10:11,008
You fools.

116
00:10:11,368 --> 00:10:13,808
You can't use justice as an excuse for murder.

117
00:10:14,208 --> 00:10:17,688
Recently, the government has been forced to change its way of thinking.

118
00:10:18,508 --> 00:10:23,548
For example, rather than fearing Pluton's revival, they have decided to awaken it.

119
00:10:23,808 --> 00:10:28,408
To use the ancient weapon as a force of justice that can bring an end to the great pirate era.

120
00:10:28,408 --> 00:10:32,468
And now, for some reason, you want to stand in their way.

121
00:10:33,048 --> 00:10:38,088
It's almost as if you have no sympathy for all of the people constantly being victimized by pirates.

122
00:10:38,588 --> 00:10:41,088
You should reconsider where your loyalties lie.

123
00:10:41,408 --> 00:10:42,348
Don't kid yourself.

124
00:10:42,908 --> 00:10:46,348
If the weapon is restored, the world is going to fight over its power.

125
00:10:46,888 --> 00:10:48,708
It will only breed more destruction.

126
00:10:50,088 --> 00:10:53,048
Are you saying you don't trust the government, Mr. Iceberg?

127
00:10:53,688 --> 00:10:55,088
That would be a shame.

128
00:10:55,088 --> 00:10:56,788
Not at all.

129
00:10:56,868 --> 00:10:58,748
It's human nature that I don't trust.

130
00:10:59,128 --> 00:10:59,488
Luchi.

131
00:11:09,488 --> 00:11:10,868
Show some respect.

132
00:11:11,228 --> 00:11:13,088
Remember, you're not my boss anymore.

133
00:11:13,088 --> 00:11:23,648
Kaku, check his pulse.

134
00:11:29,648 --> 00:11:30,468
Excuse me.

135
00:11:37,408 --> 00:11:38,608
Here's the situation.

136
00:11:38,608 --> 00:11:42,848
A while ago, we came up with a bit of a hypothesis about the blueprints.

137
00:11:43,948 --> 00:11:44,888
You needn't worry.

138
00:11:45,468 --> 00:11:49,768
Although we could torture you, we have more effective means of extracting the truth.

139
00:11:50,628 --> 00:11:55,768
All you have to do is lie there and listen to our hypothesis without saying a word.

140
00:11:58,048 --> 00:12:01,848
Even if your mouth won't cooperate, your pulse will tell us the truth.

141
00:12:01,848 --> 00:12:31,408
The wind is out to get you.

142
00:12:31,408 --> 00:12:32,948
Better be careful.

143
00:12:33,968 --> 00:12:34,288
Hey!

144
00:12:36,688 --> 00:12:37,688
Hey, Granny!

145
00:12:38,008 --> 00:12:39,028
Are we going back?

146
00:12:39,108 --> 00:12:40,068
The storm's coming!

147
00:12:40,568 --> 00:12:41,188
Oh, yeah?

148
00:12:42,128 --> 00:12:46,168
Seems like a perfect time for a stroll around town, if you ask me.

149
00:12:46,988 --> 00:12:49,648
Gives me a little time to sober up.

150
00:12:50,188 --> 00:12:50,588
Oh?

151
00:12:51,248 --> 00:12:53,128
Then why aren't you still drinking, Granny?

152
00:12:53,128 --> 00:12:58,288
You can't sober up and booze it up at the same time, can you?

153
00:12:59,728 --> 00:13:00,848
Sure I can.

154
00:13:01,028 --> 00:13:03,628
I haven't been drinking all my life for nothing.

155
00:13:04,168 --> 00:13:04,648
Huh?

156
00:13:05,228 --> 00:13:06,348
All your life?

157
00:13:06,648 --> 00:13:07,668
That's not true!

158
00:13:07,668 --> 00:13:22,768
It hasn't been all my life.

159
00:13:23,528 --> 00:13:25,468
It's only been eight years.

160
00:13:25,468 --> 00:13:40,788
Now that we're all comfortable, let me explain the thought process leading to our hypothesis.

161
00:13:41,648 --> 00:13:46,408
First of all, the way that you allowed us to retrieve the fake blueprints is highly suspicious.

162
00:13:47,148 --> 00:13:55,028
After you decided that the culprits were related to the government, you intentionally revealed the location of the fakes, presumably in the hopes of fooling us.

163
00:13:55,628 --> 00:14:01,768
However, what if your assumption was wrong and the culprit's intention was to kill you rather than to retrieve the blueprints?

164
00:14:01,768 --> 00:14:09,548
In that case, the fakes would be useless, the real blueprints would be lost, and the will of the original shipwrights would not be passed on.

165
00:14:10,388 --> 00:14:12,268
Surely you considered that possibility.

166
00:14:12,768 --> 00:14:14,068
I know you're no fool.

167
00:14:14,708 --> 00:14:20,808
And yet, despite creating a decoy, you've seemingly made no attempt to pass off the actual blueprints tonight.

168
00:14:21,568 --> 00:14:24,348
That must mean you've already entrusted them to someone.

169
00:14:24,348 --> 00:14:30,868
We still don't know where they are, but we can assume that you don't have them with you anymore.

170
00:14:31,248 --> 00:14:32,808
That's our hypothesis.

171
00:14:35,068 --> 00:14:36,028
Uh-uh.

172
00:14:36,388 --> 00:14:37,428
No squirming.

173
00:14:38,068 --> 00:14:39,748
This is still just a theory.

174
00:14:40,648 --> 00:14:44,328
I admit, we haven't found any decisive evidence.

175
00:14:44,848 --> 00:14:50,848
And despite the fact that we've kept you under surveillance for the past five years, we've never seen you do anything suspicious.

176
00:14:50,848 --> 00:14:57,148
Assuming you did entrust the blueprints to someone, they would have to be an accomplished shipwright to make any sense out of them.

177
00:15:00,988 --> 00:15:05,548
Now, if we add that to our theory, things start to get interesting.

178
00:15:06,328 --> 00:15:10,868
For example, let's take a closer look at the fake blueprints that you left us.

179
00:15:12,248 --> 00:15:16,088
Here at the bottom, you can see the signatures of three different shipwrights.

180
00:15:16,748 --> 00:15:19,348
Tom, Iceberg, and Cuddy Flan.

181
00:15:19,348 --> 00:15:22,068
And the company name, Tom's Workers.

182
00:15:23,228 --> 00:15:30,308
You probably thought that this detail wouldn't mean anything to whoever took the bait, but you had no idea your enemies were so close.

183
00:15:30,808 --> 00:15:33,348
That was a grave miscalculation on your part.

184
00:15:33,888 --> 00:15:41,988
If the person who broke in had been an outsider, once they found out that the blueprints had nothing to do with Pluton, they would probably have thrown them away.

185
00:15:41,988 --> 00:15:49,228
However, since we've been shipwrights ourselves for the past five years, the names of the designers are very interesting.

186
00:15:49,228 --> 00:15:53,588
There's a visitor, sir, to see you.

187
00:15:53,588 --> 00:16:02,048
Although many people know of the legendary shipwright Tom, the details of his actual company are shrouded in mystery.

188
00:16:03,048 --> 00:16:08,028
It's believed to have existed on this island, but there's no roster or register to be found.

189
00:16:08,028 --> 00:16:12,528
It was no easy task finding out that you were one of Tom's apprentices.

190
00:16:12,528 --> 00:16:24,608
What few reports we have indicate that he taught only two shipwrights, and a government official verified that one of them died in an accident eight years ago.

191
00:16:25,608 --> 00:16:26,548
Real shame.

192
00:16:26,548 --> 00:16:31,928
I know Cuddy Flam was a bit of a loose cannon, but I sure hate to see him go.

193
00:16:34,588 --> 00:16:39,448
It struck a chord, I thought to myself. Now, where have I heard that name before?

194
00:16:41,468 --> 00:16:46,608
He said he's a friend from years back. Do you know anyone named Cuddy Flam?

195
00:16:47,848 --> 00:16:52,508
It was about four years ago, shortly after we started our undercover operation.

196
00:16:52,508 --> 00:17:01,208
He was a man who has passed through Galilaw's gates many times since, but only once did he ever use that name.

197
00:17:01,808 --> 00:17:03,668
I remember it clearly.

198
00:17:03,928 --> 00:17:04,728
So do I.

199
00:17:06,288 --> 00:17:07,888
Shall I show him in, sir?

200
00:17:08,768 --> 00:17:10,988
No. Turn him away.

201
00:17:10,988 --> 00:17:25,768
In other words, Tom's second apprentice, Cuddy Flam, is still alive.

202
00:17:26,568 --> 00:17:28,808
And living in this town, no less.

203
00:17:34,128 --> 00:17:36,188
But now he goes by Frank.

204
00:17:40,988 --> 00:17:43,988
And what's with all the secrecy? Something going on?

205
00:17:48,788 --> 00:17:52,728
Hey, what are these? The blueprints for that old battleship?

206
00:17:53,568 --> 00:17:55,868
Don't call yourself Cuddy Flam anymore.

207
00:17:57,548 --> 00:18:00,648
It's only a matter of time before the government targets me.

208
00:18:01,648 --> 00:18:04,868
I want you to take these and leave this island, Frankie.

209
00:18:06,288 --> 00:18:08,208
Seems pretty obvious now, huh?

210
00:18:08,208 --> 00:18:13,488
I can't believe it took us all these years to figure out the connection.

211
00:18:14,968 --> 00:18:16,868
Our searches on him turned up nothing.

212
00:18:17,148 --> 00:18:20,528
I must admit that his true identity was a well-hidden secret.

213
00:18:21,108 --> 00:18:25,288
And because he's an oaf and a ship dismantler, we never really gave him much thought.

214
00:18:25,808 --> 00:18:27,028
It's ironic, really.

215
00:18:27,148 --> 00:18:27,528
What's the price?

216
00:18:27,528 --> 00:18:28,028
How dare you?

217
00:18:28,028 --> 00:18:41,048
گ

218
00:21:55,447 --> 00:22:07,447
疲れ果てた武器の中でも変わらないもの探すコンバスで いつまでも持っていれるから

219
00:22:07,447 --> 00:22:20,447
いつか辿り着く夢の終わりに できるだけこのままで大人に 見上げた果てしないこの大空に 永遠に 永遠に

220
00:22:20,447 --> 00:22:31,787
幼き頃の夢物語 忘られち記憶の糸辿り 未だ胸を焦がす残り火を消してしまわないように

221
00:22:31,787 --> 00:22:42,647
乱れた羽渡るこの空に 仲間と共に想いを託し 移り変わる時代を越え 永遠に 永遠に

222
00:22:50,447 --> 00:22:53,487
an ovalみたいなものに

223
00:22:53,487 --> 00:22:56,947
ラグだったけど 相手の群集を反映して 車のための部分を見つけたみたい

224
00:22:56,947 --> 00:22:58,447
・別に 良いです

225
00:22:58,447 --> 00:23:01,887
はい 微見にしましできれません そこまで共に臨成し

226
00:23:01,887 --> 00:23:18,727
もう驚

